{"sections":[{"type":"Meta","title":"Adur's employment, unemployment and economic inactivity - ONS","description":"How Adur (E07000223) compares with the South East and Great Britain across employment-related statistics","contentTitle":"Labour market robo reports","releaseDate":"20240514","lastUpdated":"2024-05-14","outputSeries":"labourmarketindicatorsbyunitaryandlocalauthoritiesgreatbritain","contentType":"robo-journalism"},{"type":"Header","title":"Employment, unemployment and economic inactivity in Adur","standfirst":"How Adur compares with the South East and Great Britain across employment-related statistics","label":"Select a different local authority"},{"type":"Notice","content":"<p>This pilot publication is no longer being updated. Feedback to help us develop future publications can be sent to our <a href = \"mailto: subnational@ons.gov.uk\">subnational analysis team</a>.</p>"},{"id":"section-top","type":"Summary","title":"In Adur:","content":"<a name=\"section-top\"></a>","sections":[{"indicator":"Employment rate","value":"77.6%","note":"ages 16 to 64","content":"<p>Employment in Adur has stayed at around the same rate as the previous year. This estimate is based on a small number of survey responses, which means it is likely to be less precise.</p><p><a href=\"#employment-rate\">Go to employment rate section</a>  &darr;</p>"},{"indicator":"Unemployment rate","value":"3.1%","note":"ages 16+","content":"<p>Unemployment (people looking for work) has risen since a year earlier. The unemployment rate for Adur was higher than across the South East as a whole.</p><p><a href=\"#unemployment\">Go to unemployment rate section</a>  &darr;</p>"},{"indicator":"Claimant Count","value":"2.7%","note":"ages 16 to 64","content":"<p>Claimant Count remained similar in March 2024 compared with a year earlier. The <a href=\"https://www.ons.gov.uk/employmentandlabourmarket/peopleinwork/employmentandemployeetypes/methodologies/aguidetolabourmarketstatistics#claimant-count\" rel=\"external\">Claimant Count</a>  measures the number of people who are claiming unemployment-related benefits.         </p><p><a href=\"#claimant-count\">Go to Claimant Count section</a>  &darr;</p>"},{"indicator":"Economic inactivity","value":"22.4%","note":"ages 16 to 64","content":"<p>Economic inactivity refers to people who are neither employed nor seeking work. This estimate is based on a small number of survey responses, which means it is likely to be less precise.</p><p><a href=\"#inactivity\">Go to economic inactivity section</a>  &darr;</p>"}]},{"id":"caveat","type":"Warning","content":"<p> These estimates are less precise than national or regional figures because they are based on smaller numbers of survey respondents.</p><p> Local population changes also mean that the numbers of people in each area who are employed, unemployed and economically inactive in this article may be under- or over-estimates.</p>"},{"type":"survey","content":"<p>Estimates of employment and economic inactivity are based on the <a href= \"https://www.ons.gov.uk/employmentandlabourmarket/peopleinwork/employmentandemployeetypes/methodologies/annualpopulationsurveyapsqmi\" rel=\"external\">Annual Population Survey</a>. Unemployment estimates are modelled to improve their precision compared to those based only on responses provided via the Annual Population Survey.</p><p>The Claimant Count is based on administrative data from the benefits system. These data are experimental statistics.</p>"},{"id":"employment-rate","title":"Employment in Adur has stayed at around the same rate as the previous year","content":"<p>Employment estimates for Adur are based on a small number of responses to our survey and are likely to be less precise than figures for other local areas. These estimates are presented for information only, and any conclusions drawn should include a consideration of this lack of precision. </p><p>Of people aged 16 to 64 years living in Adur, 77.6% were employed in the year ending December 2023. This is around the same rate as the year ending December 2022 when the local rate was 77.5%.</p><p>Across the South East in the year ending December 2023, 79.3% of people aged 16 to 64 years were employed. This was higher compared with the previous year, when 78.1% of people were employed.</p>"},{"id":"employment-level","content":"<p>Overall, there were around 32,000 people aged 16 and over in Adur who were employed in the year ending December 2023. This is down from the previous year, when there were around 33,900 people aged 16 and over who were employed.</p><p>Local population changes mean that the numbers of people in each area who are employed, unemployed and economically inactive in this article may be under- or over-estimates.</p>"},{"type":"end","content":"<p><a href=\"#section-top\">Back to top</a></p>"},{"id":"unemployment","title":"Unemployment rose in Adur compared with the previous year","content":"<p>Unemployment estimates are modelled to improve their precision compared to those based only on responses provided via the Annual Population Survey.</p><p>Around 1,000 people aged 16 and over in Adur were unemployed in the year ending December 2023. This is a rate of 3.1%.</p><p>This was an increase compared with the year ending  December 2022 when the unemployment rate was 2.6%.</p><p>Across the South East, from the year ending  December 2022 to the year ending December 2023, there was a decrease in the unemployment rate from 3.1% to 2.9%.</p><p>Year on year, the number of people unemployed in the South East fell from around 148,000 to around 142,000 over the same period.</p><p>Unemployment across Great Britain rose slightly between the year ending December 2022 and the year ending  December 2023, going from around 1,180,000 people (3.6%) to around 1,240,000 (3.7%).</p><p>Unemployed people are those without jobs who are actively seeking work and available to take up a job. Some jobless people may not seek work or be able to work for various reasons, such as retirement, sickness, or study, and are called \"economically inactive\". </p><p>This means that the employment rate and the unemployment rate can both go up at the same time as people move into the labour market and become job seekers.</p><p>The unemployment rate is the number of unemployed people out of everyone \"economically active\" in the labour market, that is, everyone who is employed or looking for work. The employment rate is out of the working-age population including  \"economically inactive\" people. </p>"},{"id":"chart-highest","type":"Chart","chartType":"bar-highlight","title":"Highest unemployment rates (%) of areas in the South East, with Adur for comparison","subtitle":"Among those aged 16 and over and active in the labour market, year ending December 2023","footer":"Source: Model-based estimates of unemployment from the Office for National Statistics","data":[{"x":4.6,"y":"Eastbourne"},{"x":4.5,"y":"Southampton"},{"x":4.5,"y":"Folkestone and Hythe"},{"x":4,"y":"Thanet"},{"x":4,"y":"Hastings"},{"x":3.1,"y":"Adur"}],"selected":"Adur","padding-left":"120","xSuffix":"%"},{"type":"end","content":"<p><a href=\"#section-top\">Back to top</a></p>"},{"id":"claimant-count","title":"Claimant Count in Adur was around the same as in the previous year","content":"<p>The <a href=\"https://www.ons.gov.uk/employmentandlabourmarket/peopleinwork/employmentandemployeetypes/methodologies/aguidetolabourmarketstatistics#claimant-count\" rel=\"external\">Claimant Count</a> is an experimental statistic that measures the number of people who are claiming unemployment-related benefits.</p><p>The Claimant Count in Adur was 1010 in March 2024, down from March 2023, when there were 1035 claimants.</p><p>The proportion of people aged 16 to 64 years in Adur who were claiming unemployment-related benefits was 2.7% in March 2024, a similar value compared with March 2023 when it was 2.7%.</p>"},{"id":"chart-claimanttime","type":"Chart","chartType":"line-highlight"